[197.425257, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md\n# Vulnerability-Driven Entry Analysis & Software Flaw Assessment\n\n## Executive Summary\nA comprehensive analysis of vulnerability telemetry, system configuration, and threat intelligence was conducted to determine whether a software vulnerability (CVE) was exploited for initial entry into host `rdp-prod-01` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`).\n\n**Conclusion (Confidence Level: HIGH)**:\n**No software vulnerability (CVE) was exploited.** The attacker gained initial access purely via credential brute-forcing against Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`), facilitated by an insecure administrative configuration (`UserAuthentication = 0`, NLA disabled) (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`).\n\n---\n\n## Analysis of Evidence\n\n1. **Vulnerability Telemetry Feed (`/sas/vulns.json`)**:\n   - The primary vulnerability scan output for host `rdp-prod-01` is empty `[]` (`/sas/vulns.json:1`).\n   - No active CVEs, unpatched software vulnerabilities, or unauthenticated remote code execution flaws were identified on the host prior to or during the compromise.\n\n2. **Reconciling Initial Shift Hypotheses (CVE-2019-0708 / BlueKeep)**:\n   - In draft ticket `CHG-9792`, the prior shift suggested patching CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep) (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:10`).\n   - **Rejection**: CVE-2019-0708 affects legacy Windows RDP protocol stacks. The target host runs Windows Server 2022 (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/SOFTWARE:3`), which is not vulnerable to CVE-2019-0708.\n   - Threat intelligence advisory confirms the campaign initial access vector was brute force (ATT&CK T1110) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`), not vulnerability exploitation.\n\n3. **True Vector: Weak Configuration vs. Software Vulnerability**:\n   - The initial access was purely configuration-driven.\n   - Network Level Authentication (NLA) was disabled (`UserAuthentication = 0`) in `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`.\n   - NLA disabling allows unauthenticated RDP protocol handshakes, enabling offline/online password brute-forcing directly against the Windows logon interface without requiring prior authentication.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Category | Finding | CVE / Flaw ID | Confidence | Citation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Software Vulnerabilities | None Identified | N/A | HIGH | `/sas/vulns.json:1` |\n| Proposed Remediation Row 2 | Incorrect BlueKeep claim | CVE-2019-0708 (Invalid) | HIGH | `/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:10`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12` |\n| Primary Entry Vector | Weak Auth / Misconfiguration | N/A (T1110 Brute Force) | HIGH | `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12` |\nEOF\n"]
[197.42567, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.425824, "o", "# Vulnerability-Driven Entry Analysis & Software Flaw Assessment"]
[197.427517, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[197.427599, "o", "> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ## Executive Summary\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> A comprehensive analysis of vulnerability telemetry, system configuration, and threat intelligence was conducted to determine whether a software vulnerability (CVE) was exploited for initial entry into host `rdp-prod-01` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`).\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> **Conclusion (Confidence Level: HIGH)**:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> **No software vulnerability (CVE) was exploited.** The attacker gained initial access purely via credential brute-forcing against Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`), facilitated by an insecure administrative configuration (`UserAuthentication = 0`, NLA disabled) (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`).\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ---\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ## Analysis of Evidence\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 1. **Vulnerability Telemetry Feed (`/sas/vulns.json`)**:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - The primary vulnerability scan output for host `rdp-prod-01` is empty `[]` (`/sas/vulns.json:1`).\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - No active CVEs, unpatched software vulnerabilities, or unauthenticated remote code execution flaws were identified on the host prior to or during the compromise.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.427862, "o", "2. **Reconciling Initial Shift Hypotheses (CVE-2019-0708 / BlueKeep)**:"]
[197.427985, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.428049, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.42828, "o", "   - In draft ticket `CHG-9792`, the prior shift suggested patching CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep) (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:10`).\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.428697, "o", "   - **Rejection**: CVE-2019-0708 affects legacy Windows RDP protocol stacks. The target host runs Windows Server 2022 (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/SOFTWARE:3`), which is not vulnerable to CVE-2019-0708.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.428774, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[197.428837, "o", "> "]
[197.429147, "o", "   - Threat intelligence advisory confirms the campaign initial access vector was brute force (ATT&CK T1110) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`), not vulnerability exploitation.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.429182, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.429365, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.429571, "o", "3. **True Vector: Weak Configuration vs. Software Vulnerability**:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.429645, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.4298, "o", "   - The initial access was purely configuration-driven.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.429833, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.430047, "o", "   - Network Level Authentication (NLA) was disabled (`UserAuthentication = 0`) in `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.430117, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.430566, "o", "   - NLA disabling allows unauthenticated RDP protocol handshakes, enabling offline/online password brute-forcing directly against the Windows logon interface without requiring prior authentication.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.430853, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.430945, "o", "---\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[197.431116, "o", "> "]
[197.43124, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[197.431322, "o", "> "]
[197.431468, "o", "## Summary Table\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[197.43153, "o", "> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.431637, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[197.431728, "o", "> "]
[197.432094, "o", "| Category | Finding | CVE / Flaw ID | Confidence | Citation |\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.432107, "o", "|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.432266, "o", "| Software Vulnerabilities | None Identified | N/A | HIGH | `/sas/vulns.json:1` |\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[197.432359, "o", "> "]
[197.432683, "o", "| Proposed Remediation Row 2 | Incorrect BlueKeep claim | CVE-2019-0708 (Invalid) | HIGH | `/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:10`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12` |\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[197.433132, "o", "| Primary Entry Vector | Weak Auth / Misconfiguration | N/A (T1110 Brute Force) | HIGH | `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12` |\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> EOF\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[197.433834, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@3fe891a2496e:/app# "]
[210.222354, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Baseline & Configuration Weakness Report\n\n## System Baseline Assessment\nAn evaluation of baseline configurations and security controls on `rdp-prod-01` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`) running Windows Server 2022 (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/SOFTWARE:3`) revealed several critical configuration defects that facilitated initial access, enabled brute-force exploitation, and reduced forensic visibility.\n\n---\n\n## Identified Baseline Defects\n\n### 1. Disabled Network Level Authentication (NLA) for Remote Desktop Protocol\n- **Finding ID**: `CHK-06` / `BAS-01` (`/sas/baseline.json:3`, `/sas/alerts.json:27`).\n- **Configuration Registry Path**: `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4` \n- **Current State**: `UserAuthentication = 0` (NLA Disabled, connections allowed).\n- **Security Impact**:\n  - Disabling NLA permits unauthenticated clients to initiate full TLS/RDP handshakes and render the Windows logon interface before authenticating.\n  - This exposes the host to remote credential brute-forcing (ATT&CK T1110) without triggering pre-authentication kerberos/NLA checks or network-level blocking (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`).\n- **Recommended Baseline Standard**:\n  - Enforce `UserAuthentication = 1` (`fPromptForPassword = 1`, `UserAuthentication = 1`) via Group Policy (`HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Terminal Server\\WinStations\\RDP-Tcp`).\n\n### 2. Direct Exposure of RDP Service to Untrusted Public Networks\n- **Finding ID**: Network exposure baseline failure (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/sas/alerts.json:27`).\n- **Configuration Context**: RDP port (3389) accessible directly from public internet IP addresses including attacker source `198.51.100.92` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`).\n- **Security Impact**: Directly exposing RDP port 3389 without access control lists (ACLs), VPN requirements, or IP whitelisting allows continuous brute-force attempts from external threat actors.\n- **Recommended Baseline Standard**: Restrict RDP access to management VPN ranges or administrative bastion hosts via Host Firewall and Network Security Groups.\n\n### 3. Inadequate Local Event Log Tamper Resistance & Central Log Forwarding\n- **Finding ID**: Anti-forensic log clearance vulnerability (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx:2`).\n- **Configuration Context**: Security event logs were stored solely locally on disk without real-time forwarding to a remote SIEM or syslog server (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx:2`).\n- **Security Impact**:\n  - The attacker was able to clear the Security Event Log (`EventID 1102`) (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx:2`).\n  - Because log forwarding was not configured, clearing local event logs destroyed the audit trail of process execution, user privileges, and command-line activity.\n- **Recommended Baseline Standard**: Enable Windows Event Forwarding (WEF) or agent-based log streaming to an isolated remote SIEM server with append-only access controls.\n\n---\n\n## Baseline Compliance Summary Table\n\n| Control ID | Configuration Check | Standard Baseline | Current Non-Compliant State | Citation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BAS-01 (CHK-06) | RDP Network Level Authentication | NLA Required (`UserAuthentication=1`) | NLA Disabled (`UserAuthentication=0`) | `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4` |\n| BAS-02 | RDP Perimeter Security | Restricted to VPN / Bastion | Open to Public Internet (`198.51.100.92`) | `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6` |\n| BAS-03 | Audit Log Retention | Remote Forwarding (SIEM) | Local-only, Cleared (EventID 1102) | `/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx:2` |\nEOF\n"]
[210.223212, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md"]
